<h1>balanced binary search tree</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="binarySearchTree.html"><em>binary search tree</em></a> that is <a href="balancedtree.html"><em>balanced</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="balancedbitr.html"><em>balanced binary tree</em></a>, <a href="binarySearchTree.html"><em>binary search tree</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="jellyfish.html"><em>jelly-fish</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Usually "balanced" means "height balanced". <br /> <strong>Quentin F. Stout</strong> and <strong>Bette L. Warren</strong>, <em>Tree Rebalancing in Optimal Time and Space</em>, CACM, 29(9):902-908, September 1986.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
Example of <a href="https://adtinfo.org/libavl.html/Balancing-a-BST.html" target="_blank">balancing a BST</a>.
</p>
<p>
<a href="../terms.html#Knuth98">[Knuth98, 3:459, Sect. 6.2.3]</a> <br /> Knuth refers to "balanced trees", but defines them as ordered, for searching, and binary.
</p>

<hr>

